refined_question:
For each s ∈ (1/2, 3/2), let γ := 1 - 1/(2s), and let L_{γ,1,s} be the optimal constant in the critical fractional Lieb–Thirring inequality for the self-adjoint operator (-Δ)^s + V on L^2(R),
Tr(((-Δ)^s + V)_-)^{γ} ≤ L_{γ,1,s} ∫_R V_-(x) dx,
valid for all real-valued potentials V with V_- ∈ L^1(R). If L^{(1)}_{γ,1,s} denotes the corresponding one-particle constant, i.e. the optimal constant in the inequality obtained by replacing the left-hand side by |λ_1(( -Δ)^s + V)|^{γ} (equivalently, the contribution of a single negative eigenvalue), is it true that
L_{1-1/(2s),1,s} = L^{(1)}_{1-1/(2s),1,s}
for every s ∈ (1/2, 3/2)? ([link.springer.com](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00220-024-05216-y))

Let Ω be a bounded spatial domain with periodic boundary conditions, and for a detection kernel g of perceptual radius R define f_{g,R}(x,t) = ∫_Ω f(y,t) g(x−y) dy. Consider the three consumer-resource formulations from §2.2.2:
(2.14) u_t = D1Δu − γ∇·(u∇v_{g,R}) + cβuv/(α+v) − du,  v_t = D2Δv + rv(1−v/K) − βuv/(α+v);
(2.18) u_t = D1Δu − γ∇·(u∇q_{g,R}) + cβuv/(α+v) − du,  v_t = D2Δv + rv(1−v/K) − βuv/(α+v),  q_t = bv − μq;
(2.19) u_t = D1Δu − γ∇·(u∇q_{g,R}) + cβuv/(α+v) − du,  v_t = D2Δv + rv(1−v/K) − βuv/(α+v),  q_t = buv − (μ+ξu)q,
where u is consumer density, v is resource density, q is the cognitive map, γ,r,K,β,α,d,c,b are positive, and μ,ξ are nonnegative. Assuming spatially non-constant steady-state solutions exist, how do their qualitative features differ among (2.14), (2.18), and (2.19)? In particular, how does replacing direct perception of current resource density by a dynamic cognitive map that grows either with v alone or with uv and decays at rate μ or μ+ξu alter the resulting spatially non-constant steady-state profiles?

Let P(x1, ..., xn) be a non-constant polynomial with integer coefficients. Determine whether the set of integer solutions
S(P) = {(x1, ..., xn) in Z^n : P(x1, ..., xn) = 0}
is finite or infinite; if S(P) is finite, determine all integer solutions.

Let (x_n)_{n\ge 1} be a nonincreasing sequence of positive real numbers with \sum_{n=1}^\infty x_n < \infty, and for each n let r_n := \sum_{m>n} x_m. Assume that x_n < r_n for every n \in \mathbb{N}. Must there exist an infinite subsequence (x_{n_k})_{k\ge 1}, with n_1 < n_2 < \cdots, such that its achievement set
E(x_{n_k}) := \left\{\sum_{k=1}^\infty \varepsilon_k x_{n_k} : \varepsilon_k \in \{0,1\}\right\} \subset \mathbb{R}
is a Cantorval?
